  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nCurrently, the trough axis extends just to our east with ridging \nstretched across the spine of the Rockies. The 540 height line \ncurrently encompasses the entire forecast area with 534 heights \nbuilding in from the north. The area is on the western edge of an \nexpansive surface high centered over the Great Lakes region. Very \nlight easterly winds over the region, along with mostly clear \nconditions, are allowing efficient radiational cooling to occur this \nmorning. Temperatures across central and southeast Kansas range \nbetween 20-25 degrees. \n\nAs we move through the day and into Thursday, the upper trough will \nbecome an upper level cut-off low to our east. A surface low will \ndeepen over the Eastern US and Gulf moisture will surge north early \non Thursday. It is possible on Thursday morning that light \nprecipitation in the form of flurries impacts southeast Kansas. At \nthis time, model consensus keeps moisture and precipitation east of \nthe forecast area across Missouri. If precip does occur, it will \nremain extremely light due to the dry air mass in place and no \nimpacts are expected. Until this upper level system fully exits the \nregion to the east, below normal temperatures will persist through \ntoday and Thanksgiving with afternoon highs only in the 40s and lows \ntonight dipping into the lower to middle 20s once again. \n\nBroad ridging will finally begin to build in over the Plains by \nFriday leading to increasing thickness. While we will remain just on \nthe eastern periphery of the ridge, we can expect warmer conditions \nthrough the end of the forecast period with slightly above normal \ntemperatures continuing this weekend and into early next week. By \nFriday afternoon highs will reach into the middle to upper 50s and \nhighs around 60 will be seen for Saturday. A short wave associated \nwith a broad upper trough sweeping across the northern tier of the \ncountry will just graze Kansas on Saturday. There continues to be \nsome model discrepancy with the GFS keeping the forecast area dry \nwhile the ECMWF introduces some sprinkles or light rain showers into \nthe forecast on Saturday night. High temperatures in the 50s and dry \nconditions will then continue through the end of the forecast period \nwith locations in southern Kansas approaching 60 many\nafternoons.",
